# Text files are stored LF in the index and checked out LF on every platform.
#
# Why: the index was already ~225 of 637 tracked files CRLF, so any code that
# reads a repo file and compares it against a literal containing \n was silently
# sensitive to how the reader cloned the repo. Git for Windows defaults to
# core.autocrlf=true, so a Windows clone put CRLF on disk where a Linux clone
# put LF. #458 fixed the two known instances and added a guard, but a
# normalised repository removes the whole class of bug rather than the two
# instances that happened to be noticed. See #476.
#
# The one-time normalisation of the files already in the index is a separate
# commit, to be made when the merge queue is empty -- it touches ~225 files and
# would collide with every open pull request. This file only sets the policy
# going forward.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-native scripts keep CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Recorded byte-for-byte: these are recordings, not source. Their line endings
# are part of the recorded bytes and must not be converted by anyone, ever.
# The conformance goldens are compared as bytes, and the context-window figures
# were measured against a shipped release.
tests/conformance/snapshots/*.snap -text
docs/Context-Window-Analysis.md -text
